===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|ang|slītan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*slītaną||to tear apart}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*(s)leyd-||to tear, [[rend#Verb|rend]] (cut apart), split apart}}. Possibly cognate with {{cog|la|laedo|laed-|to strike, hurt, injure}}. {{doublet|en|slite}}; also related to {{m|en|slice}} through French borrowing.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-slit.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪt|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[narrow]] [[cut]] or [[opening]]; a [[slot]].
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=17|passage=The face which emerged was not reassuring. It was blunt and grey, the nose springing thick and flat from high on the frontal bone of the forehead, whilst his eyes were narrow '''slits''' of dark in a tight bandage of tissue.{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|vulgar|slang}} The [[vulva]].
# {{lb|en|vulgar|slang|derogatory}} A woman, usually a sexually loose woman; a [[prostitute]].

